{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790923944,"nonce":"e+RuRkb+j13YxYBA+L2eAsOpP5o=","title":"There is no payload-mapping catalog, so every operator writes their incident tool's mapping from scratch and nothing stops a shipped mapping from resolving on quiet","message":"**What is wrong.** ADR 0055 §2: \"A **catalog** of community mappings is a folder of files — contributed, reviewed and imported onto a Connection; no code ships with one.\" §4: \"**oto's own code sends no command, and no mapping in oto's catalog turns a fact into a resolve, close or status change**; a catalog review refuses one.\" There is no catalog folder, no import, and no check. A human reviewer is the only thing §4 currently rests on, and a reviewer reading a Liquid branch on `reason == \"quiet\"` can miss a `\"resolve\"` three lines down.\n\n**The demonstration.** The two vendors this ticket starts with both have a resolve in the same field that carries every ordinary event, which is exactly why the check has to be mechanical. PagerDuty Events v2: `event_action` is one of `trigger`, `acknowledge`, `resolve` (https://docs.pagerduty.com/developer/send-alert-event). incident.io HTTP alert events: `status` is one of `firing`, `resolved`, and is required (https://docs.incident.io/openapi/tags/alert-events-v2.json). A mapping that writes `{% if envelope.incident.state == \"quiet\" %}resolve{% else %}trigger{% endif %}` is one line and reads as helpful.\n\n**Why it matters.** ADR 0055's Consequences make \"adding a tool is a pull request of one file\" the whole vendor strategy. A catalog without a mechanical §4 check turns oto's own shipped files into the thing ADR 0052 §5 refused — \"It **never** sends PagerDuty's `resolve` or any close or status change\" — and the docs ticket's warning that quiet is not fixed would be contradicted by oto's own examples.\n\n**Where.** A top-level folder of mapping files (not under `internal/`; one file per vendor, in the same document format 2205620 stores on a Connection, each carrying its vendor's docs URL and the date its field names were checked), embedded in the binary so a deployment can list it; an endpoint and a Settings → Connections control that list the catalog and import one file onto a webhook Connection by copying it (a copy, never a reference: a later catalog change must not alter a live Connection); a Go test over the folder.\n\n*The catalog check.* Each catalog file declares its vendor's command fields and their forbidden values (for PagerDuty, `event_action` ∉ {`resolve`, `acknowledge`}; for incident.io, `status` ≠ `resolved`). The test renders every catalog file against the sample envelope for every one of the 20 reasons (the corpus 2205620 builds for save-time validation) and fails if any rendered body carries a forbidden value. A file that declares no command field is refused rather than passed, so a reviewer is forced to name it. Import does not re-run the check (an operator may edit an imported mapping into a command — §4 makes that their rule), but the catalog itself can never ship one.\n\n*The two starter mappings, verified 2026-10-02 against the vendors' current public docs:*\n\n1. **incident.io HTTP alert source.** `POST https://api.incident.io/v2/alert_events/http/{alert_source_config_id}`; body fields `title` (required), `status` (required, `firing` | `resolved`), `deduplication_key`, `description`, `source_url`, `metadata`; success is `202` with `deduplication_key`, `message`, `status` (https://docs.incident.io/openapi/tags/alert-events-v2.json). The token goes \"in either the query string or the 'Authorization' header\" (https://docs.incident.io/api-reference/alert-sources-v2/create); the mapping uses the Connection's bearer credential, and ⚠️ the implementer must confirm the `Bearer` prefix against a live source, because the docs imply the scheme without stating it. The mapping sends `status: firing` for every fact, `quiet` included, and `deduplication_key` = `incident.id`.\n2. **PagerDuty Events API v2.** `POST https://events.pagerduty.com/v2/enqueue`; required `routing_key`, `event_action`, `payload.summary` (≤ 1024 chars), `payload.source`, `payload.severity` (`critical` | `error` | `warning` | `info`); optional `dedup_key` (≤ 255 chars), `payload.custom_details`, `links` (https://docs.pagerduty.com/developer/send-alert-event); success is `202` with `status`, `message`, `dedup_key` (https://docs.pagerduty.com/developer/api/reference/events-v2/send-event/create-v2-event). The mapping sends `event_action: trigger` for every fact with `dedup_key` = `incident.id`. `severity` comes from the member alerts' own `severity` label in the envelope, never a value oto invents (ADR 0052 §5); the mapping must state what it sends when no member carries one, and say so in the file.\n\n⛔ **The PagerDuty mapping has an unsolved problem this ticket does not decide.** `routing_key` travels in the BODY. A mapping is non-secret configuration, and 2205620 persists the rendered body into `notification_deliveries.rendered` before the send, so a routing key written into a mapping is plaintext in the Connection config, in every delivery row and in the audit — against the rule that \"a credential belongs in channel_credentials, sealed and rotatable, not in a config blob\" (`internal/channels/providers/webhook/headers.go:21-23`). ADR 0055 does not say where a body-borne secret lives. Do not ship a PagerDuty mapping that holds the key. If 2205620 or a follow-up has given a mapping a sealed-credential reference that is substituted after persistence, use it; otherwise ship incident.io plus a second vendor whose credential rides the Connection's existing basic or bearer credential, verify that vendor's fields against its current public docs the same way and cite the URLs in the file, and comment here naming PagerDuty as waiting on that decision.\n\nNeither vendor's alert endpoint returns an external incident URL or id — only the de-duplication key oto sent. A starter mapping's response path therefore names `external_id` at most, and `external_url` not at all; 506ff21's echo will show nothing for these two. Say so in each file rather than map a field that does not exist.\n\n**Scope.** medium.\n\n**Done when.** The catalog folder holds two mapping files, each with its docs URLs and checked-on date; the catalog test renders every file against all 20 reasons and passes, and a fixture file that maps `quiet` to `resolve` (or to `status: resolved`) fails it, as does a file declaring no command field; Settings → Connections lists the catalog and imports a file onto a webhook Connection as a copy, which then passes save-time validation and the test send; no catalog file contains a credential.\n\n**Blocked by.** 2205620.\n\n**Mode.** AFK. Source: ADR 0055 §2 and §4, ticket-filing session 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}